    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> sacred kingdom whose priest-king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a>, held ritual rather than military authority over much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a>.</li><li>Nri agents marked with ichi scars cleansed abominations, settled disputes and conferred Ozo titles across a wide area.</li><li>Its traditional history begins in 948; in 1911 British officials forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a> to renounce his ritual powers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A dignitary buried at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> had been buried seated on a wooden stool; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a>, too, were buried seated, in a wood-lined chamber.</p></aside>
    </section>

